---
import Article from '../../components/Article.astro';

const title = 'Spider vs FreeCell: Which Solitaire Should You Play? | CardHearth';
const description =
  'Spider or FreeCell? One hides cards and builds in suit across two decks; the other deals everything face up and is almost always winnable. An honest comparison to pick your game.';
const updated = '2026-06-24';
const heading = 'Spider vs FreeCell';
const subtitle =
  'Two of the most-played solitaires, and almost total opposites — here is how they differ and which one fits how you like to play.';

const breadcrumb = [
  { name: 'CardHearth', href: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', href: '/guides/' },
  { name: 'Spider vs FreeCell', href: '/guides/spider-vs-freecell/' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is FreeCell easier than Spider?',
    a: 'Usually, yes. Almost every FreeCell deal is winnable and nothing is hidden, so with careful play your win rate can be very high. Spider — especially at two and four suits — has hidden cards and unscreened deals, so a meaningful share simply cannot be won. One-suit Spider is the exception and is beginner-friendly.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which is more about skill?',
    a: 'Both reward planning, but in different ways. FreeCell is a pure open puzzle: all the information is on the table, so a loss is nearly always a misplay. Spider mixes skill with the luck of what the face-down cards turn out to be, so even perfect play loses some deals.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which should a beginner start with?',
    a: 'Start with FreeCell or one-suit Spider. FreeCell teaches deliberate, plan-ahead play with almost every deal winnable; one-suit Spider teaches the build-and-clear rhythm without the punishing suit rules. Save two- and four-suit Spider for once those habits are second nature.',
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'Spider vs FreeCell: Which Solitaire Should You Play?',
    description,
    datePublished: updated,
    dateModified: updated,
    author: { '@type': 'Person', '@id': 'https://cardhearth.com/#jared', name: 'Jared Waterworth', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/about/' },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'CardHearth',
      url: 'https://cardhearth.com/',
      logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://cardhearth.com/logo.png' },
    },
    mainEntityOfPage: 'https://cardhearth.com/guides/spider-vs-freecell/',
    image: 'https://cardhearth.com/og.png',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: breadcrumb.map((c, i) => ({
      '@type': 'ListItem',
      position: i + 1,
      name: c.name,
      item: `https://cardhearth.com${c.href}`,
    })),
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Article
  title={title}
  description={description}
  heading={heading}
  subtitle={subtitle}
  updated={updated}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
  cta={{ href: '/freecell/', label: 'Play FreeCell' }}
>
  <div class="tldr">
    <p class="tldr-title">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Play <a href="/freecell/">FreeCell</a></strong> if you want an open puzzle where almost every deal is winnable and losing means you misplayed.</li>
      <li><strong>Play <a href="/spider/">Spider</a></strong> if you want hidden cards, two decks, and the satisfying grind of building suit runs — with luck as part of the challenge.</li>
      <li><strong>New to both?</strong> Start with FreeCell or one-suit Spider.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    <a href="/spider/">Spider</a> and <a href="/freecell/">FreeCell</a> are both wildly popular, but
    they sit at opposite ends of the solitaire spectrum. The quickest way to tell them apart: FreeCell
    shows you everything and is almost always winnable, while Spider hides cards, uses two decks, and
    leaves some deals genuinely unbeatable. Here's how that plays out.
  </p>

  <h2>How they're dealt</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>FreeCell</strong> deals all 52 cards <em>face up</em> into eight cascades, plus four free
    cells as workspace. There's no stock and nothing hidden — the whole puzzle is in front of you from
    move one. <strong>Spider</strong> uses <em>two</em> decks (104 cards) across ten columns, most of
    them face down, with a stock that deals a new row whenever you're stuck. You don't know what you're
    sitting on until you uncover it.
  </p>

  <h2>The goal</h2>
  <p>
    In FreeCell you build the four foundations Ace-to-King by suit, using alternating-colour stacking
    in the cascades along the way. In Spider you don't build foundations at all — you assemble complete
    King-to-Ace runs <em>in a single suit</em> right in the columns, and each finished run is removed.
    Clear all eight runs and you win. That single-suit rule is what makes Spider's difficulty scale so
    sharply with the suit count.
  </p>

  <h2>Difficulty and winnability</h2>
  <p>
    This is the biggest practical difference. FreeCell is <strong>almost always winnable</strong> — of
    the classic 32,000 numbered deals only <a href="/freecell/deal/11982/">#11982</a> has no solution —
    so a loss is nearly always a misplay you can learn from. Spider's deals are <strong>not screened for
    solvability</strong>: one-suit is forgiving, but <a href="/spider-2-suits/">two suits</a> and
    especially <a href="/spider-4-suits/">four suits</a> include deals that cannot be won no matter how
    you play. Our <a href="/guides/are-solitaire-games-winnable/">are solitaire games winnable?</a> guide
    has the full breakdown.
  </p>

  <h2>Which should you play?</h2>
  <p>
    Choose <strong>FreeCell</strong> for a calm, fair, plan-it-all-out puzzle where skill is everything —
    it's the best game on the site for getting genuinely better. Choose <strong>Spider</strong> when you
    want a longer, more atmospheric grind with the tension of hidden cards and the luck of the draw.
    Many players keep both in rotation: FreeCell to sharpen up, Spider to unwind. Dig deeper with the
    <a href="/guides/freecell-strategy/">FreeCell strategy guide</a> and the
    <a href="/guides/spider-solitaire-strategy/">Spider strategy guide</a>, or compare FreeCell with the
    other classic in <a href="/guides/freecell-vs-klondike/">FreeCell vs Klondike</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {
    faq.map((f) => (
      <details class="faq">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold">{f.q}</summary>
        <p class="mt-2 opacity-90">{f.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))
  }
</Article>
